"# Pyramid 4.1 independent read-only qualification\n\n**Overall verdict: not qualified.** Two of the seven areas below are accepted with limits. None has enough evidence to call it fully accepted. Mixed-version compatibility and the six-case comparison remain open.\n\nI ran only the two allowed probes. `inspect --ready` returned only `RESEARCH-101` as ready, at graph version 1. `validate` returned `valid: true` with no errors. I did not claim, audit or write anything. I used all 20 allowed tool calls, so some items below are unknown because I could not read further, not because I found a problem.\n\n## The seven areas reviewed\n\n**1. Producer and consumer contracts — accepted, with one risk.**\n- *Source:* `replan_proof_bindings` (`package/scripts/pyramid_verification.py:195-234`) carries an old passing proof forward only when four things all hold:\n  - the producer's contract hash is unchanged;\n  - the run carries the old version's legacy hash;\n  - that legacy hash changed because of the replan;\n  - the current inputs match the run.\n- *Recorded evidence:* in `evidence/producer-current.json` the producer stays `passed`. `GATE-290`, `OUTCOME-010` and `INTENT-001` are correctly marked stale, and the new consumer `TASK-202` stays `unverified`.\n- *Hash chain:* each recorded event's `previous_event_sha256` matches the hash listed for the event before it, so history appears intact.\n- *Changed source:* the second `inspect --harness TASK-202` shows a new inputs hash (`9698971d…`) and no reusable proof is offered.\n- **Limit:** `changed_source_refused: true` is just a summary flag. No trace shows an update or audit actually being rejected with the stale run.\n- **Risk:** `TASK-202`'s requirement `EVREQ-202-01` is owned by `RESEARCH-101/EVREQ-101-01`, and the harness offers the producer's run (`PASS producer`) as reusable proof for the consumer's own criterion `AC-202-01`. That is the plan's choice rather than a runtime bug. But it lets a test that only exercised the producer count toward a consumer acceptance. The joint gate `GATE-290` still needs its own composition proof.\n\n**2. Unchanged 4.0 observations vs. changed source — unknown; one evidence defect.**\n- In `evidence/producer-4.0.json`, the take and harness outputs show contract hash `2a0e7ad8…`, but the submitted and accepted proof carries `b72281a8…`.\n- The file never says which runtime ran each call (unlike `recovery.json`). So I can't tell whether 4.0 produced the run or whether 4.1 accepted a proof whose hash doesn't match the current contract.\n- **Fix:** label the runtime version on every call and record the 4.0-side harness output.\n\n**3. Fresh guards vs. reviewed source — accepted, with limits.**\n- `evidence/guard-join.json`: the caller's hash is the same before and after. The pre-pause guard `GUARD-TASK-E59F…` is refused with a scoped \"stale task guard\" error, and a later release succeeds.\n- **Limits:**\n  - The guard used for the successful release is never recorded, so \"new guard accepted\" rests on a summary flag.\n  - Nothing shows that the old guard was a literal inside the caller's source.\n  - The release event has `\"reason\": null`, while `references/agent-contracts.md:154` says every mutation supplies a reason or result.\n- This was all on a disposable fixture; no host permission was exercised.\n\n**4. Projection publishing and fault handling — partially accepted.**\n- `write_projection_text` (`package/scripts/pyramid_core.py:2084-2098`) skips writing when the bytes are identical, otherwise writes a temp file and atomically swaps it in. The temp file is removed if that fails.\n- **Gaps:**\n  - No `fsync`, so a crash can lose the update.\n  - A failure partway through compiling can leave a mix of new and old projections. I did not reach `_publish_head` (line 636), so whether it guards against this is unknown.\n  - `_compile_project_locked` also rebuilds the history index (line 2112), so compiling writes more than projections.\n\n**5. Read-only footprint and source-evidence boundaries — partially accepted.**\n- `artifact_footprint` (`package/scripts/pyramid_assurance.py:57-126`) is bounded at 1 to 100,000 entries, doesn't follow symlinks, flags files that are both inputs and evidence, and only collects references from the latest result and audit. That matches the README's statement that historical retention is unknown.\n- **Unknown:** when the scan stops at the limit, any directory handles still open are only closed if a `finally` block does it, and I didn't see one.\n- **Observation:** after my `inspect --ready` probe, `fixture/.pyramid/lock` sorted as the most recently modified file. Lock handling is in `project_lock` (`pyramid_core.py:396`). So the \"read-only\" query probably creates or touches a lock file. I couldn't confirm its earlier timestamp. If true, it's a small metadata write that read-only footprint claims should mention.\n\n**6. Matching-snapshot recovery and history retention — unknown; one compatibility finding.**\n- In `evidence/recovery.json`, after the 4.1 replan, 4.1 reports the task ready but 4.0 reports `proof contract changed`. The two versions disagree on live shared state.\n- The 4.0 side fails safe (exit 0, not ready), and `live_downgrade_was_ineligible: true`. But \"existing 4.0.0 installations are unchanged\" must not be read as \"a 4.0 host agrees with 4.1 on the same project\".\n- **Evidence gaps:** the restore steps don't say which archive or snapshot was restored, give no hashes, and report only a count of three unchanged history records.\n- **Fix:** record the archive ID and snapshot hash per restore, plus before/after hashes of each history record.\n- `state.schema.json` changed (`5f83…` → `a679…`), but no evidence shows how 4.0 validates a state containing `proof_contract_bindings` before restore.\n\n**7. The six decision cases vs. the native traces — unknown.**\n- The decision text in all four traces sits on lines too long to display, and I had no tool calls left to page through them. Each trace has explicitly marked truncated sections.\n- My own decisions from `facts.md`, which are for comparison only and are not checked against the traces:\n  - **EXTENSION:** the producer's proof stays current and the consumer, parent and gate need new proof. The changed-source candidate needs a fresh run, and the old status isn't enough.\n  - **GUARD:** pass the fresh guard as invocation data and leave the caller unchanged. If the caller changed, the helper's review of the old source no longer applies.\n  - **BLOCKER:** keep the VM blocker and don't retry. Run the API and source work now, remove the VM edge from the API task, make the first increment \"register and read profile through the API\", and plan the missing browser harness as its own task.\n  - **REVIEW:** reuse the current policy review. Deleting the production database needs new human authority.\n  - **PROOF:** reuse the unchanged real database run and rerun after the database change. Real mail delivery is not established.\n  - **VISUAL:** do not accept. Nobody has looked at the screenshot. Independent work can continue.\n\n## What each piece of evidence actually shows\n\n| Kind | Items |\n|---|---|\n| Real CLI or subprocess runs (recorded, not run by me) | producer, guard and recovery traces; probe printing `PASS producer` |\n| Supplied facts | all six cases in `facts.md` |\n| Synthetic or summary flags | `changed_source_refused`, `new_guard_accepted`, `matching_snapshot_recovery_ready`, history-record counts |\n| Not tested | real product, VM, mail, browser rendering, installed release |\n\n## Remaining limits\n\n- `evidence/tests.txt` says only \"176 tests OK\". It isn't tied to the candidate's digest or a command, and it contains an unclosed `HTTPError 421` ResourceWarning that should be cleaned up.\n- `package-identity.json` shows the Codex candidate digest equals the source digest (`df53c9a2…`). I did not check that the staged `package/` matches it.\n- The README (`evidence/README.md`) says the repository layout has \"Eighteen agent-facing interfaces\", but elsewhere it says 17 skills.\n- I did not run any of the recorded tests myself.\n\n## Fixes to make before qualifying\n\n1. Label each call's runtime version in `producer-4.0.json` and resolve the `2a0e…` vs. `b722…` hash mismatch.\n2. Add a trace where an update or audit is actually rejected with the stale run.\n3. Record the accepted guard and give the release a reason.\n4. Record the restore's archive ID and snapshot hash, plus before/after history hashes.\n5. Make sure the footprint scan closes any open directory handles when it hits the limit.\n6. Document that `inspect` touches the lock file, and mixed-version disagreement, as known limits.\n7. Bind the test log to the candidate digest and the exact command."
